# 1️⃣ Correlated Subqueries

## Big Idea

A correlated subquery is a subquery that depends on the outer query.

👉 Normal subquery runs once.
👉 Correlated subquery runs for EACH row.

---

## Mental Model 🧠

```text id="e1e3p6"
Normal subquery:
Run once

Correlated subquery:
Run repeatedly for every row
```

---

# Example Table

## Employees

| id | name   | dept | salary |
| -- | ------ | ---- | ------ |
| 1  | Akshay | CSE  | 50000  |
| 2  | Rahul  | CSE  | 60000  |
| 3  | Neha   | ECE  | 70000  |

---

# Problem

👉 Find employees earning ABOVE department average.

---

# SQL Solution

```sql id="n0x2f0"
SELECT e1.name, e1.salary, e1.dept
FROM employees e1
WHERE e1.salary >
(
 SELECT AVG(e2.salary)
 FROM employees e2
 WHERE e1.dept = e2.dept
);
```

---

# Step-by-Step Thinking 🧠

For Akshay:

* Find avg salary of CSE
* Compare Akshay salary

For Rahul:

* Again find avg of CSE
* Compare Rahul salary

For Neha:

* Find avg of ECE

Subquery depends on current row.

---

# Why Called “Correlated”

Because:

```text id="t1s0hz"
Inner query references outer query
```

Here:

```sql id="lm3o2n"
e1.dept = e2.dept
```

---

# Python Analogy 🧠

```python id="1m0t0z"
for e1 in employees:

    dept_salaries = [
        e2["salary"]
        for e2 in employees
        if e2["dept"] == e1["dept"]
    ]

    avg_sal = sum(dept_salaries) / len(dept_salaries)

    if e1["salary"] > avg_sal:
        print(e1["name"])
```

Nested repeated logic.

---

# Memory Trick 🧠

```text id="y1st7s"
Correlated Subquery = inner query depends on outer row
```

---

# Performance Note ⚠️

Correlated subqueries can be slower because query runs repeatedly.

Sometimes JOIN/window functions are faster.

Interview favorite point.

